/* exercises */
const EX=[['Circuits','Kirchhoff / Thevenin','V1 = 10 V → 1k → node A; 1 mA source from A to ground; A → 0.5k → 0.5k → V2 = 5 V (− on the circuit side). Find I_B (through the 0.5k + 0.5k branch) and the voltage across the current source.','Open the 0.5k branch: R_TH = 1k + 0.5k = 1.5k, V_TH = 9 V. I_B = (9 + 5)/(1.5k + 0.5k) = 14 V/2k = <b>7 mA</b>, I_A = 8 mA, V_source = <b>−2 V</b>. Load it in Maker (preset “Back to basics”).'],['Circuits','Thevenin on a bridge-like net','V1 = 10 V feeds R1 = 1k; R2 = 1k goes from that node to ground; R3 = 1k is in series with a 5 mA current sink to ground; load R4 = 1k sits across R3 + sink. Find V_R4.','R_TH = R1‖R2 = 500 Ω (R3 doesn’t matter). V_TH = V_R3 − V_sink = 5 V − 2.5 V = 2.5 V. Voltage divider: V = 2.5·1/(1.5) = <b>1.67 V</b>.'],['Circuits','Two sources, one load','30 V–6k–(4k to ground)–5k load–(4k to ground)–6k–20 V. Find I_L.','R_TH = 2·(6k‖4k) = 4.8k. V_TH = 12 V − 8 V = 4 V. I_L = 4/(4.8 + 5) = <b>0.41 mA</b>. If R_L changes, only the last step changes.'],['Diodes','Current source + diode','10 mA source → ideal diode → 1k. Find the diode Q point.','The source forces the diode ON: Q = <b>(0 V, 10 mA)</b>; the resistor drops 10 V.'],['Diodes','Two batteries and a diode','5 V behind 1k and 5 V behind 10k, diode across the node. Find the diode state in both polarities.','Thevenin between A and K: R_TH = 909 Ω, |V_TH| = 4.09 V. Reverse polarity (V_TH = −4.09 V): <b>OFF</b>, Q = (−4.09 V, 0). Flipped: ON, I = 4.09/0.909 = <b>4.5 mA</b> (3.4 mA if the diode drops 1 V).'],['Diodes','Zener in the same circuit','V1 = 5 V (1k), V2 = 15 V (10k), zener V_Z = 5 V across the node.','Assuming OFF gives a Q outside the curve (impossible). Assuming forward: I1 = 1.5 mA, I2 = 5 mA, so I_D = 5 − 1.5 = <b>3.5 mA</b>. Correct assumption.'],['Diodes','Zener with the load line','Thevenin: R_TH = 500 Ω, V_TH = 2.5 V, zener V_Z = 5 V. What changes if V_Z = 1 V?','Load line points: (V_AK = 0, I = −5 mA) and (V_AK = −2.5 V, 0). V_Z = 5: the zener is <b>OFF</b> at (−2.5 V, 0). V_Z = 1: zener region, V_AK = −1 V, I = (−2.5 + 1)/500 = <b>−3 mA</b>.'],['Diodes','Real diode 0.7 V','V_TH = 5 V, R_TH = 2k, diode V_F = 0.7 V.','Forward region, no assumption needed: I_D = (5 − 0.7)/2k = <b>2.15 mA</b>. With a 3.3 V zener the other way round: zener region, I = (5 − 3.3)/2k = <b>0.85 mA</b>.'],['Diodes','LED resistor and battery life','LED V_F = 2.5 V on 10 V, limit to 10 mA. Then a 15 V / 100 Wh battery feeds LED + 100 Ω + 5 V zener in series.','R = (10 − 2.5)/10 mA = <b>750 Ω</b>. I = (15 − 2.5 − 5)/100 = 75 mA, P = 1.125 W, time = 100/1.125 ≈ <b>89 h</b>.'],['MOSFET','Gate divider','V_DD = 15 V, gate divider R1 = 10k (top), R2 = 1k (bottom), R3 = 100 Ω load. Curves: cutoff for V_GS ≤ 4 V.','V_GS = 15·1/11 = 1.36 V: <b>cutoff</b>, I_D = 0, V_DS = 15 V.'],['MOSFET','LED switch','V_GS = 6 V, 10 V supply, R_D = 100 Ω, LED 1.8 V. Curve V_GS = 6 V: 100 mA at 1 V knee.','R_on = 1 V/100 mA = 10 Ω. Saturation would need 82 mA &lt; 100 mA, so: ohmic. I = 8.2/(100 + 10) = <b>74.5 mA</b>, V_DS = 0.75 V.'],['MOSFET','Pick R for 100 mA','5 V supply, LED 2 V, V_GS ≈ 5 V, curve: 6 A at 3 V (R_on = 0.5 Ω).','V_DS = 0.1·0.5 = 0.05 V. R = (5 − 2 − 0.05)/0.1 = <b>29.5 Ω</b>. P_MOSFET = 0.05·0.1 = <b>5 mW</b>.'],['BJT','Operation point, β = 100','V_CC = 15 V, V_BB = 5 V. (a) R_B = 430 Ω, R_C = 300 Ω. (b) R_B = 4.3k, R_C = 100 Ω.','(a) I_B = 4.3/0.43 = 10 mA → βI_B = 1 A ≫ 50 mA: <b>saturation</b>, I_C = 50 mA, V_CE ≈ 0. (b) I_B = 1 mA, I_C = 100 mA, V_CE = 15 − 10 = <b>5 V, active</b>.'],['BJT','LED switch design','V_CC = 15 V, V_BB = 5 V, LED 3 V, I_LED = 100 mA, β = 100. Find R1 (base) and R2 (collector).','R2 = (15 − 3)/0.1 = <b>120 Ω</b>. Saturation needs I_B ≥ 1 mA; take 2 mA: R1 = (5 − 0.7)/2 mA ≈ <b>2.2 kΩ</b>.']];
